# [CBOR](../wg/cbor.html)

## Summary

The CBOR Working Group met at IETF 127. The session focused primarily on resolving feedback from the ongoing IETF Last Call for `draft-ietf-cbor-serialization` and addressing second Working Group Last Call feedback for `draft-ietf-cbor-edn-literals`. Key technical discussions covered CDDL serialization control operators, designated expert guidance regarding CBOR tag registries, serialization requirements for framework protocols, reintroducing restricted ellipsis semantics in diagnostic notation, and finalizing prefix and specification naming conventions.

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Status and Agenda
- Paul Hoffman presented the [CBOR 127 chair slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-cbor-cbor-127-chair-slides-02), reviewing the Note Well guidelines, meeting logistics, and agenda.

### CBOR Serialization (`draft-ietf-cbor-serialization`)
- Lawrence Lundblade presented [Summary of draft-ietf-cbor-serialization](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-cbor-summary-of-draft-ietf-cbor-serialization-00). The presentation highlighted that the draft simplifies serialization choices by establishing "Preferred Plus" as a straightforward choice for most non-streaming applications, clarifies ambiguities in RFC 8949 (STD 94), and provides a direct path to deterministic serialization (e.g., via map key sorting).
- **CDDL Serialization Control Operators**:
  - Christian Amsüss noted an issue raised during designated expert review regarding the `.serialization` CDDL control operator: as currently described, it operates on CBOR data items prior to serialization, which introduces metadata tracking into CDDL productions. He suggested either specifying the control on the serialized byte string or removing the operator to address it in a broader CDDL annotation specification.
  - Carsten Bormann agreed that introducing serialization controls into CDDL without a comprehensive annotation mechanism creates challenges for CDDL implementers.
  - Lawrence Lundblade expressed openness to removing or refactoring the operator while emphasizing that expressing deterministic serialization constraints in CDDL remains desirable.
- **Section 7 and Designated Expert (DE) Guidance on Tag Unification**:
  - Christian Amsüss raised concerns regarding Section 7 guidance on grandfathered Tags 2 and 3 (bignums) and the interpretation of RFC 8949 Section 3.4.3 across basic versus extended generic data models.
  - Carsten Bormann emphasized that tag design rules affecting the integer data model require precise DE guidance so future tag definitions do not repeat past integration issues.
  - Area Director Andy Newton clarified that DE guidance must be recorded in an RFC rather than relying solely on IANA web page notes to prevent loss during registry updates.
- **Guidance for Framework Protocols**:
  - Rohan Mahy questioned the recommendation discouraging framework protocols (such as COSE or SD-JWT) from imposing serialization requirements using RFC 2119 keywords.
  - Paul Hoffman and Lawrence Lundblade agreed that using RFC 2119 `RECOMMENDED` / `SHOULD` is inappropriate here; framework protocol designers should instead be advised to explicitly evaluate their serialization needs.
- **Document Structure and Content**:
  - Rohan Mahy suggested moving normative definitions (Preferred Plus, deterministic serialization) earlier in the document. Lawrence Lundblade agreed to prepare a pull request for this reorganization.
  - Rohan Mahy raised the option of removing or shortening Appendices C, H, I, and J. Paul Hoffman and the working group preferred retaining them for historical and implementation context.

### Extended / Concise Diagnostic Notation (`draft-ietf-cbor-edn-literals`)
- **Reintroducing Ellipsis (`...`)**:
  - Christian Amsüss proposed restoring ellipsis (`...`) syntax with restricted semantics: permitted in diagnostic notation for partial CBOR debugging and documentation/examples, but triggering a non-parse error when converting to CBOR binary unless explicitly overridden by tool flags.
  - Rohan Mahy requested prohibiting ellipsis within quoted string grammars.
  - The working group supported bringing back ellipsis under these restricted terms, and Christian Amsüss agreed to draft text.
- **Encoding Indicators & Application Extensions**:
  - Rohan Mahy noted missing semantics and examples for encoding indicators applied to prefixed strings and application sequence extensions in Table 7, offering to submit proposed text.
- **Naming Decisions**:
  - Carsten Bormann presented [Diagnostic Notation: Postponed Naming Decisions](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-cbor-diagnostic-notation-postponed-naming-decisions-00) to resolve naming for the specification and application extension prefixes.
  - **Specification Name**: Carsten Bormann outlined options between EDN (Extended Diagnostic Notation) and CDN (Concise Diagnostic Notation / CBOR Diagnostic Notation). Rohan Mahy, Martine Lenders, Christian Amsüss, and Dave Robin favored retaining the established name "EDN" to avoid churn across external specifications that reference it. Paul Hoffman noted the naming decision would be confirmed on the mailing list.
  - **Application Extension Prefixes**: There was broad agreement to update temporary application extension prefixes `b1` -> `b` (byte string) and `t1` -> `t` (text string). For indefinite-length prefixes, proposals such as `indef-b` and `indef-t` were discussed.

### Future Topics
- Paul Hoffman noted that discussions on dCBOR and packed CBOR will be scheduled in upcoming interim meetings once `draft-ietf-cbor-serialization` and `draft-ietf-cbor-edn-literals` complete their last call processes.

